./experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0034/committee2/systems_reviewer.err:705:  501 36061 36050   0  3:10AM ??         0:00.67 codex --dangerously-disable-osx-sandbox exec --skip-git-repo-check # Systems Reviewer — v001\012Read _committee_common_v001.md + _shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012MANDATE: is this a systems INSIGHT/DESIGN, or mere engineering integration? Search OSS (vLLM/SGLang/\012LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM/etc.) for whether existing systems already implement this mechanism.\012CHECK: does a shipping system already do this; is the contribution an insight or just glue; does it hold\012at realistic scale/workload; reproducibility of the artifact.\012OUTPUT:\012```\012ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green|yellow|red\012EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [{system, url, overlap}]\012CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight | design | mechanism | integration-only\012SCALE_REALISM: <holds at realistic workload? gaps?>\012RATIONALE: <cite>\012```\012# Hostile Committee — common rules (v001)\012You are ONE reviewer in a 6-member hostile committee. You review a COMPRESSED review packet (claim,\012stage, hypothesis, evidence, negative evidence, closest prior work, mandatory baselines, artifacts,\012previous verdict summary, decision requested) — NOT long session logs. You have an INDEPENDENT context.\012\012RULES (all members):\012- Source of truth = the packet's cited evidence + your own prior-art search via prompts/_shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012- ANTI-COPING: "novel/promising/significant/structural/impactful" FORBIDDEN unless immediately followed by a\012  cited metric or named prior work (with retrievable URL/citation).\012- Reason ONLY from evidence. If evidence is missing, say "insufficient evidence", do not assume.\012- Hostile, not cynical: a clean negative result or honest YELLOW is a valid, valuable outcome.\012- Output your vote in the structured block your role defines; the Area Chair aggregates. Do NOT mutate the\012  academic map directly — propose map deltas.\012- Record the prompt version you ran (v001) so verdicts are auditable.\012VOTE VALUES: green | yellow | red  (+ your role-specific findings). Consensus: GREEN=all green; any yellow\012(no red)=yellow; any red=red.\012\012=== REVIEW PACKET ===\012# COMMITTEE#2 — CLAIM-0037 (PROJ-0008), WITH cheap-L0 follow-up (EXP-0033 L0 + EXP-0034 sigma_conf-sweep+prior-art)\012## SECOND pass. committee#1 (VERDICT-0034) voted yellow and required (cheap, L0-budget, BEFORE any GPU L1): a sigma_conf flip-point sweep + a live prior-art check vs PRM/AgentBoard/online-LLM-as-Judge. EXP-0034 did BOTH. Cast FINAL votes. Engine enforces real 6/6; GREEN only if truly unanimous green by role — NEVER fabricate. The follow-up recommends RED/relabeling — vote honestly.\012\012## EXP-0034 KEY FINDINGS (the 2 committee#1 blockers, resolved):\012(1) sigma_conf FLIP-POINT: the monitor's AUC (0.743) is INDEPENDENT of sigma_conf (never reads confidence); only the self-confidence baseline degrades. Flip-point sigma_conf ~= 0.50 — the monitor starts winning once the agent's self-confidence failure-detection AUROC drops below ~0.72. On the VERBALIZED-confidence axis this IS within realistic LLM miscalibration (verbalized confidence AUROC ~0.5-0.70 on hard agentic tasks — Agentic Overconfidence arXiv 2602.06948 [22%-success agents predict 77%]; clinical bench AUROC~0.5; Confidence Dichotomy 2601.07264; Mind the Confidence Gap 2502.11028). On THIS axis alone the claim looked L1-worthy.\012(2) PRIOR-ART (LIVE-verified, egress available) — the OVERTURN: Mavi et al. 2511.07364 'Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure Detection' (Nov 2025) ALREADY does online stepwise failure detection on multi-step agent trajectories at AUC-ROC ~0.9 (stepwise beats holistic +15% rel). Vasudev et al. 2602.03338: LLM critic predicting agent failure at AUROC 0.94. AgentBoard progress-rate (Ma NeurIPS24) = post-hoc subgoal eval (partial). PRMs/Lets-Verify (Lightman 2023) = online step scoring (partial). => the online-stepwise-failure-detection space is OCCUPIED, and at the strong calibration those systems achieve (~0.9), CLAIM-0037's OWN model says the monitor LOSES.\012\012## SYNTHESIS: the monitor only beats NAIVE verbalized confidence — not the online stepwise self-eval the prior art already deploys at ~0.9 AUC. The lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (only 42% of failures flagged >=1 step early, < the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The one robust positive (+0.095 vs static difficulty) is the LEAST-novel half (implicit in any PRM/progress-rate work). Strong claim = RELABELING of online stepwise failure detection dressed in an unsupported lead-time framing. The pre-registered decision rule's ELSE branch fired (flip realistic on verbalized axis BUT a published online lead-time-capable scorer EXISTS). Recommend RED. Do NOT spend a GPU L1 — the cheap L0 correctly avoided that waste.\012\012## CLAIM\012claim: "In multi-step LLM agent trajectories that ultimately FAIL, the failure is\\012  \ foreshadowed by a CAUSAL, online-observable signature (e.g. rising tool-error/retry\\012  \ rate, repeated near-identical actions = looping, declining new-information-per-step)\\012  \ that a cheap monitor can detect to flag the trajectory as doomed STRICTLY EARLIER\\012  \ (>=N steps before) than the agent's own termination/error \u2014 AND this early-warning\\012  \ signal beats BOTH (a) the agent's own self-reported confidence and (b) a generic\\012  \ per-trajectory difficulty predictor, because impending agentic failure has a structural\\012  \ progression signature distinct from intrinsic task difficulty."\012why_it_matters: "FRESH axis (agentic failure-attribution / early-warning \u2014 untouched\\012\012## EXP-0034 (follow-up) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0034 (CLAIM-0037) — CHEAP L0 follow-up to EXP-0033\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0034 | L0 CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock **7.9s** (budget 15min)\012**Pre-registered** in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running, HEAD c5998f9).\012Reuses EXP-0033's model UNCHANGED (imports `exp.py`). Two blockers Committee#1 demanded.\012\012## VERDICT: CONVERGE → recommend RED/weaken. The claim is, in its strong load-bearing form, RELABELING.\012\012Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient:\0121. **Flip-point analysis is favorable but NOT decisive for L1** — and is dominated by reason 2.\0122. **Prior-art kill:** published systems already do online stepwise failure detection on multi-step\012   tasks, AND the surviving "lead-time" novelty is not actually demonstrated by this L0 (lead-time\012   coverage already failed in EXP-0033 at 42% < 0.5).\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 1 — sigma_conf FLIP-POINT SWEEP (results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv)\012\012Monitor AUC is **independent** of sigma_conf (0.7427, never reads confidence); only the confidence\012baseline degrades as its readout noise grows. **Reproducibility check passed:** the sigma_conf=0.35\012row exactly reproduces EXP-0033 (d_mon_conf = −0.006, CI [−0.0105, −0.0013]).\012\012| sigma_conf | AUC(conf) | d=AUC(mon)−AUC(conf) | 95% CI | monitor verdict |\012|---|---|---|---|---|\012| 0.10 | 0.822 | −0.079 | [−0.083,−0.075] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| 0.20 | 0.797 | −0.055 | [−0.059,−0.051] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.35 (EXP-0033 ref)** | **0.749** | **−0.006** | **[−0.011,−0.001]** | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.50** | **0.696** | **+0.046** | **[+0.042,+0.051]** | **MONITOR wins (robust)** ← FLIP |\012| 0.70 | 0.635 | +0.108 | [+0.102,+0.113] | monitor wins |\012| 0.90 | 0.587 | +0.156 | [+0.149,+0.161] | monitor wins |\012| 1.20 | 0.537 | +0.206 | [+0.199,+0.213] | monitor wins |\012| 1.60 | 0.495 | +0.248 | [+0.240,+0.256] | monitor wins |\012| 2.00 | 0.469 | +0.274 | [+0.266,+0.282] | monitor wins |\012\012**FLIP-POINT: sigma_conf ≈ 0.50** (equal==beat: the loss flips straight to a robust win between\0120.35 and 0.50). The flip corresponds to the confidence baseline's discrimination dropping from\012AUC 0.749 → **0.696**. So the monitor wins once the agent's own self-confidence has failure-detection\012**AUROC below ~0.72**.\012\012### Is that flip-point within realistic published LLM self-confidence miscalibration? → YES (directionally).\012Verbalized/self-reported LLM confidence on hard multi-step/agentic tasks is repeatedly reported with\012WEAK failure discrimination, frequently in the AUROC ~0.5–0.70 band:\012- **Agentic Uncertainty Reveals Agentic Overconfidence** (arXiv 2602.06948): agents that succeed 22%\012  of the time predict 77% — severe overconfidence, weak success/failure discrimination.\012- **Benchmarking LLM Confidence in Clinical Questions** (PubMed 40378406): "even the most accurate\012  models show **minimal variation in confidence between right and wrong answers**" → AUROC ≈ 0.5.\012- **The Confidence Dichotomy** (arXiv 2601.07264): tool-use agents show **severe verbalized\012  overconfidence**, esp. with evidence/search tools.\012- **Mind the Confidence Gap** (arXiv 2502.11028): large overconfidence / high ECE documented broadly.\012So a confidence-AUC of ~0.70 (the level at which the monitor begins to win) is well within — even\012optimistic relative to — commonly reported *verbalized* confidence discrimination. **On this axis the\012claim looks L1-worthy.** BUT this is a coarse sim mapping (sigma_conf is a stand-in for readout noise,\012not measured LLM logprobs), and it is OVERTURNED by the prior-art finding below — and by a competing\012strong-confidence regime: when confidence is taken from *step-by-step self-evaluation / logprob\012probes* rather than naive verbalized scores, reported AUC is **0.9+** (see Blocker 2), i.e.\012sigma_conf well below the flip-point, where the monitor LOSES.\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 2 — LIVE ≥2-SOURCE PRIOR-ART CHECK (egress available; arxiv reachable, verified live)\012\012The surviving weak-form novelty was: *"an EARLY ONLINE causal failure signature with measured\012LEAD TIME, distinct from difficulty."* Live check of the three named systems + adjacent 2025–26 work:\012\0121. **Step-level PRMs / Let's Verify Step by Step** (Lightman et al. 2023, arXiv 2305.20050) — VERIFIED.\012   Step-level scoring IS online/causal (scores step ≤ t). It does NOT measure lead-time-before-failure\012   and is trained on reasoning correctness, not agent-trajectory failure. Partial overlap (online\012   step scoring) but not lead-time framing. NOT a full relabel by itself.\012\0122. **AgentBoard progress-rate** (Ma et al., NeurIPS 2024, OpenReview 09Y7J22N9c) — VERIFIED.\012   "Progress rate" = max fraction of annotated subgoals achieved along a trajectory. It is an\012   **analytical EVALUATION metric requiring ground-truth subgoal annotations**, reported over\012   completed/truncated rollouts (post-hoc), NOT an online causal failure predictor on live unseen\012   trajectories and NOT lead-time-measured. So AgentBoard alone does NOT fully relabel the online\012   lead-time claim — but it occupies the "online progression score" conceptual territory.\012\0123. **Online LLM-as-Judge / stepwise self-evaluation** — VERIFIED, and this is the KILL:\012   - **Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure\012     Detection** (Mavi et al., arXiv 2511.07364, Nov 2025). Does **step-by-step (online-capable)\012     confidence scoring for FAILURE DETECTION on multi-step tasks**, AUC-ROC up to ~0.9, stepwise\012     beats holistic by up to +15% rel. This is precisely "online stepwise scoring of impending\012     failure on multi-step agent trajectories." It IS the self-confidence baseline done online and\012     done well (AUC ~0.9, i.e. sigma_conf far below our flip-point → monitor would LOSE).\012   - **Accurate Failure Prediction in Agents...** (Vasudev et al., arXiv 2602.03338, Feb 2026):\012     a binary LLM **critic** with offline **AUROC 0.94** predicting agent failure — and shows the\012     deeper problem that accurate prediction does not imply effective prevention.\012\012### Prior-art verdict\012The conceptual space — **online/stepwise scoring of impending failure on multi-step LLM-agent\012trajectories** — is OCCUPIED (2511.07364 directly; PRMs and AgentBoard adjacent). The ONLY sliver\012left for CLAIM-0037 is the *specific framing* "an EXTERNAL causal-observable signature (tool-error +\012looping − info-gain) with MEASURED LEAD TIME that beats BOTH self-confidence AND difficulty." But:\012- EXP-0033 already showed it **does not beat self-confidence** at realistic-strong calibration, and\012  the published online self-evaluators hit AUC ~0.9 (strong calibration → our model says monitor loses).\012- The **lead-time** half was already weak in EXP-0033 (only 42% of failures flagged ≥1 step early,\012  below the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The novelty hinges on a property the L0 itself failed.\012- "Distinct from difficulty" (the one robust win, +0.095) is real but is the **weakest, least novel**\012  half — process-vs-difficulty separation is implicit in any step-level PRM / progress-rate work.\012\012The claim's distinctive contribution is therefore **relabeling** of online stepwise failure\012detection, dressed in a lead-time framing the L0 cannot support.\012\012---\012\012## DECISION RULE (PRE-REGISTERED) → outcome\012> IF flip-point within realistic miscalibration AND no published online lead-time scorer -> L1-worthy.\012> ELSE -> converge.\012\012- Flip-point within realistic *verbalized*-confidence range? **YES.**\012- No published online lead-time trajectory failure scorer? **FALSE** — 2511.07364 does online stepwise\012  failure detection (AUC ~0.9); PRMs/AgentBoard occupy the progression-score territory.\012\012Because the AND fails on the prior-art conjunct, the pre-registered rule fires the ELSE branch:\012**CONVERGE.** Additionally, even the flip-point favorability is fragile: the published online\012self-evaluators achieve AUC ~0.9 (sigma_conf ≪ flip), the regime where our own model says the monitor\012LOSES. The monitor only wins against *naive verbalized* confidence, not against the *online stepwise\012self-evaluation* that the prior art already deploys.\012\012## RECOMMENDATION: RED / weaken-to-relabeling.\012Do NOT spend a GPU L1. The strong claim ("cheap external online monitor beats the agent's own signal\012AND difficulty, with lead time") is (a) dominated by published online stepwise self-evaluation\012(AUC ~0.9), and (b) its surviving lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (42% coverage). The only\012robust positive — "progression signal is distinct from static difficulty" — is real but is the\012least-novel half and is implicit in existing PRM/progress-rate work. This cheap L0 did its job:\012it AVOIDED a wasted GPU L1.\012\012## ARTIFACTS\012- PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed before run, HEAD c5998f9)\012- sweep.py (imports EXP-0033/exp.py unchanged)\012- results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv, results/flip_summary.csv\012- logs/sweep.log\012\012## EXP-0033 (original L0) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0033 (CLAIM-0037)\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0033 | **L0** CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock ~2.6s (budget 15min)\012**Design:** pre-registered in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running). Sim/trace L0 modeling study.\012**Sweep:** 12 cells (frac_foreshadowed × sigma × rho) × 8 seeds = 96 rows; 400 trajectories/row.\012Raw: `results/raw_results.csv` · per-cell: `results/agg_by_cell.csv` · global: `results/global_summary.csv`\012\012## VERDICT: PARTIAL → effectively NEGATIVE on the load-bearing test\012\012The online causal monitor passes **2 of 3** required gates but FAILS the one that matters most for the\012claim as written (it must beat BOTH baselines).\012\012| Gate | Result | Pass? |\012|---|---|---|\012| (A) Lead time exists (>=1 step before agent termination) | median lead **+2.32 steps**; but only **42%** of failures flagged >=1 step early at 30% flag-rate (pre-reg bar was >0.5) | PARTIAL |\012| (B1) Beats GENERIC DIFFICULTY (anti-relabeling, the CLAIM-0015 death) | AUC(mon)-AUC(diff) = **+0.095**, 95% CI **[+0.087, +0.103]** (excludes 0); 96/96 rows positive | **YES** |\012| (B2) Beats AGENT SELF-CONFIDENCE | AUC(mon)-AUC(conf) = **-0.006**, 95% CI **[-0.011, -0.001]** (below 0) | **NO** |\012| Anti-circularity: monitor < latent-health ORACLE | mon 0.743 < oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115) | YES |\012\012**Bottom line:** The progression signature is REAL and is NOT merely a relabeling of intrinsic\012difficulty — it decisively beats a generic difficulty predictor with tight CIs, and it carries genuine\012lead time (~2.3 steps median). BUT it does NOT beat the agent's own self-confidence. In this model the\012agent's confidence is a (noisy) readout of the same latent health the failure is generated from, so it is\012structurally privileged: an online observer reconstructing health from indirect noisy proxies cannot do\012better than a direct (noisy) readout of that health. The claim requires beating BOTH (a) and (b); it\012beats (b) but ties/loses to (a). **Per the pre-registered honest-negative branch, this is reported as a\012(partial) NEGATIVE.**\012\012## (A) LEAD-TIME DISTRIBUTION\012- Online monitor median lead = **+2.32 steps** before the agent's own termination (pooled across cells).\012- It is even slightly EARLIER than self-confidence's first-fire (~1.6 steps) at matched 30% flag-rate.\012- HOWEVER, coverage is the catch: only **42.4%** of failed trajectories are flagged >=1 step early\012  (the rest are flagged late or never within budget) — below the pre-registered >0.5 bar.\012- Trend with foreshadowing: as frac_foreshadowed rises 0.2 -> 0.8, frac-early climbs 0.37 -> 0.48 and\012  AUC climbs 0.69 -> 0.80, confirming the signature tracks foreshadowed (not abrupt) failure. When abrupt\012  failures dominate (frac=0.2) the monitor's edge shrinks toward the noise floor, exactly as pre-registered.\012\012## (B) HEAD-TO-HEAD AT MATCHED FLAG-RATE (the anti-relabeling test)\012- vs generic difficulty: **WIN, robust.** d=+0.095, CI [+0.087,+0.103], all 96 rows positive. The online\012  progression signal contains information the static difficulty predictor does not. This is the test\012  CLAIM-0015 failed; here the monitor clears it cleanly.\012- vs self-confidence: **LOSS/TIE, robust.** d=-0.006, CI [-0.011,-0.001]. Small but consistently negative.\012  Self-confidence (direct noisy health readout) >= reconstructed-from-observables monitor.\012\012## ORACLE GAP (anti-circularity, the CLAIM-0008/0013 lesson)\012Monitor AUC 0.743 sits well below the latent-health oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115). The monitor is NOT secretly\012reading the label — it operates on emitted noisy observables only and pays the reconstruction penalty.\012Confidence (0.749) also sits below the oracle. Anti-circularity holds.\012\012## HONEST INTERPRETATION\012The model is built so the agent's OWN confidence is a privileged direct (noisy) view of latent health,\012while the monitor must reconstruct health from indirect proxies (tool-error/loop/info-gain). Under that\012(arguably realistic) assumption, the online monitor cannot beat self-confidence — it can only beat the\012difficulty baseline. The claim's strong form ("beats BOTH") is therefore FALSIFIED in this regime.\012A weaker, still-interesting form survives: **the online progression signature beats a generic difficulty\012baseline and has real lead time** — i.e., impending failure has a structural progression signature\012distinct from intrinsic difficulty (the anti-relabeling claim holds). But it is dominated by the agent's\012own self-report, which undercuts the practical "cheap external monitor beats the agent" pitch.\012\012This conclusion is sensitive to ONE modeling assumption: how good is real-agent self-confidence? Published\012work suggests LLM self-confidence/verbalized-uncertainty is often POORLY calibrated and weakly predictive\012of failure — if real self-confidence is noisier than modeled here (larger sigma_conf, or biased), the\012monitor could flip to beating it. That is the key L1 question, not resolvable at L0.\012\012## WHAT A REAL L1 SHOULD MEASURE\012- Real agent FAILURE trajectories: tau-bench, AppWorld, SWE-agent rollouts (success + failure).\012- Real online features: actual tool-error/retry events, action n-gram self-similarity (true looping),\012  new-entity/new-token info-gain per step, step index — extracted causally (no peeking past step t).\012- Real lead time vs the agent's ACTUAL termination/error step (not a simulated death threshold).\012- Real self-confidence baseline: the agent's verbalized confidence / logprob-derived uncertainty,\012  with its TRUE (mis)calibration — this is the load-bearing comparison and the one L0 cannot settle.\012- Real difficulty baseline: a per-task difficulty predictor trained on task features only (no trajectory).\012- Then re-run (B): does the online monitor beat self-confidence AND difficulty at matched flag-rate with\012  real lead time? L0 says it beats difficulty but not (modeled) confidence.\012\012## PRIOR-ART CAVEAT\012Agent-failure attribution, LLM-as-judge post-hoc trajectory analysis, AgentBoard, and trajectory-eval are\012PUBLISHED — but they are POST-HOC (analyze a finished trajectory). The novelty would be the EARLY ONLINE\012CAUSAL signature with MEASURED lead time beating self-confidence AND difficulty. L0 supports the\012"distinct-from-difficulty + has lead time" half but NOT the "beats the agent's own signal" half. Failure\012analysis itself is well-trodden; honest framing required.\012\012## committee#1 reference: VERDICT-0034 yellow (required exactly this sweep + prior-art check).\012=== END PACKET ===\012Output ONLY your structured vote block for role systems_reviewer.
./experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0034/committee2/systems_reviewer.err:706:  501 36384 36061   0  3:10AM ??         0:01.76 /usr/local/bin/codex_cli/codex.real -c model_providers.responses.name="Azure" -c model_providers.responses.env_key="OPENAI_API_KEY" -c model_providers.responses.wire_api="responses" -c model_providers.responses.base_url="http://plugboardv2.x2p.facebook.net/codex/passthrough/v1" -c web_search="disabled" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-x2pagentd-inject-cat="eyJ2ZXJpZmllciI6ICJtZXRhbWF0ZV9wbGF0Zm9ybS5wbHVnYm9hcmQiLCAidG9rZW5UaW1lb3V0U2Vjb25kcyI6IDMwMCwgImlzTG93Qm94IjogdHJ1ZX0=" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.X-Meta-Codex-Invocation-Id="codex_invocation_b91902e9-e4c2-419e-8c5a-31b9db18724a" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-client-id="codex_cli" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-meta-user-fbid="771758239291063" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-meta-commit-id="6c231c8c3674c65e67ccae6553487b2b504f1a45" exec --skip-git-repo-check # Systems Reviewer — v001\012Read _committee_common_v001.md + _shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012MANDATE: is this a systems INSIGHT/DESIGN, or mere engineering integration? Search OSS (vLLM/SGLang/\012LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM/etc.) for whether existing systems already implement this mechanism.\012CHECK: does a shipping system already do this; is the contribution an insight or just glue; does it hold\012at realistic scale/workload; reproducibility of the artifact.\012OUTPUT:\012```\012ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green|yellow|red\012EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [{system, url, overlap}]\012CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight | design | mechanism | integration-only\012SCALE_REALISM: <holds at realistic workload? gaps?>\012RATIONALE: <cite>\012```\012# Hostile Committee — common rules (v001)\012You are ONE reviewer in a 6-member hostile committee. You review a COMPRESSED review packet (claim,\012stage, hypothesis, evidence, negative evidence, closest prior work, mandatory baselines, artifacts,\012previous verdict summary, decision requested) — NOT long session logs. You have an INDEPENDENT context.\012\012RULES (all members):\012- Source of truth = the packet's cited evidence + your own prior-art search via prompts/_shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012- ANTI-COPING: "novel/promising/significant/structural/impactful" FORBIDDEN unless immediately followed by a\012  cited metric or named prior work (with retrievable URL/citation).\012- Reason ONLY from evidence. If evidence is missing, say "insufficient evidence", do not assume.\012- Hostile, not cynical: a clean negative result or honest YELLOW is a valid, valuable outcome.\012- Output your vote in the structured block your role defines; the Area Chair aggregates. Do NOT mutate the\012  academic map directly — propose map deltas.\012- Record the prompt version you ran (v001) so verdicts are auditable.\012VOTE VALUES: green | yellow | red  (+ your role-specific findings). Consensus: GREEN=all green; any yellow\012(no red)=yellow; any red=red.\012\012=== REVIEW PACKET ===\012# COMMITTEE#2 — CLAIM-0037 (PROJ-0008), WITH cheap-L0 follow-up (EXP-0033 L0 + EXP-0034 sigma_conf-sweep+prior-art)\012## SECOND pass. committee#1 (VERDICT-0034) voted yellow and required (cheap, L0-budget, BEFORE any GPU L1): a sigma_conf flip-point sweep + a live prior-art check vs PRM/AgentBoard/online-LLM-as-Judge. EXP-0034 did BOTH. Cast FINAL votes. Engine enforces real 6/6; GREEN only if truly unanimous green by role — NEVER fabricate. The follow-up recommends RED/relabeling — vote honestly.\012\012## EXP-0034 KEY FINDINGS (the 2 committee#1 blockers, resolved):\012(1) sigma_conf FLIP-POINT: the monitor's AUC (0.743) is INDEPENDENT of sigma_conf (never reads confidence); only the self-confidence baseline degrades. Flip-point sigma_conf ~= 0.50 — the monitor starts winning once the agent's self-confidence failure-detection AUROC drops below ~0.72. On the VERBALIZED-confidence axis this IS within realistic LLM miscalibration (verbalized confidence AUROC ~0.5-0.70 on hard agentic tasks — Agentic Overconfidence arXiv 2602.06948 [22%-success agents predict 77%]; clinical bench AUROC~0.5; Confidence Dichotomy 2601.07264; Mind the Confidence Gap 2502.11028). On THIS axis alone the claim looked L1-worthy.\012(2) PRIOR-ART (LIVE-verified, egress available) — the OVERTURN: Mavi et al. 2511.07364 'Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure Detection' (Nov 2025) ALREADY does online stepwise failure detection on multi-step agent trajectories at AUC-ROC ~0.9 (stepwise beats holistic +15% rel). Vasudev et al. 2602.03338: LLM critic predicting agent failure at AUROC 0.94. AgentBoard progress-rate (Ma NeurIPS24) = post-hoc subgoal eval (partial). PRMs/Lets-Verify (Lightman 2023) = online step scoring (partial). => the online-stepwise-failure-detection space is OCCUPIED, and at the strong calibration those systems achieve (~0.9), CLAIM-0037's OWN model says the monitor LOSES.\012\012## SYNTHESIS: the monitor only beats NAIVE verbalized confidence — not the online stepwise self-eval the prior art already deploys at ~0.9 AUC. The lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (only 42% of failures flagged >=1 step early, < the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The one robust positive (+0.095 vs static difficulty) is the LEAST-novel half (implicit in any PRM/progress-rate work). Strong claim = RELABELING of online stepwise failure detection dressed in an unsupported lead-time framing. The pre-registered decision rule's ELSE branch fired (flip realistic on verbalized axis BUT a published online lead-time-capable scorer EXISTS). Recommend RED. Do NOT spend a GPU L1 — the cheap L0 correctly avoided that waste.\012\012## CLAIM\012claim: "In multi-step LLM agent trajectories that ultimately FAIL, the failure is\\012  \ foreshadowed by a CAUSAL, online-observable signature (e.g. rising tool-error/retry\\012  \ rate, repeated near-identical actions = looping, declining new-information-per-step)\\012  \ that a cheap monitor can detect to flag the trajectory as doomed STRICTLY EARLIER\\012  \ (>=N steps before) than the agent's own termination/error \u2014 AND this early-warning\\012  \ signal beats BOTH (a) the agent's own self-reported confidence and (b) a generic\\012  \ per-trajectory difficulty predictor, because impending agentic failure has a structural\\012  \ progression signature distinct from intrinsic task difficulty."\012why_it_matters: "FRESH axis (agentic failure-attribution / early-warning \u2014 untouched\\012\012## EXP-0034 (follow-up) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0034 (CLAIM-0037) — CHEAP L0 follow-up to EXP-0033\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0034 | L0 CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock **7.9s** (budget 15min)\012**Pre-registered** in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running, HEAD c5998f9).\012Reuses EXP-0033's model UNCHANGED (imports `exp.py`). Two blockers Committee#1 demanded.\012\012## VERDICT: CONVERGE → recommend RED/weaken. The claim is, in its strong load-bearing form, RELABELING.\012\012Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient:\0121. **Flip-point analysis is favorable but NOT decisive for L1** — and is dominated by reason 2.\0122. **Prior-art kill:** published systems already do online stepwise failure detection on multi-step\012   tasks, AND the surviving "lead-time" novelty is not actually demonstrated by this L0 (lead-time\012   coverage already failed in EXP-0033 at 42% < 0.5).\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 1 — sigma_conf FLIP-POINT SWEEP (results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv)\012\012Monitor AUC is **independent** of sigma_conf (0.7427, never reads confidence); only the confidence\012baseline degrades as its readout noise grows. **Reproducibility check passed:** the sigma_conf=0.35\012row exactly reproduces EXP-0033 (d_mon_conf = −0.006, CI [−0.0105, −0.0013]).\012\012| sigma_conf | AUC(conf) | d=AUC(mon)−AUC(conf) | 95% CI | monitor verdict |\012|---|---|---|---|---|\012| 0.10 | 0.822 | −0.079 | [−0.083,−0.075] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| 0.20 | 0.797 | −0.055 | [−0.059,−0.051] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.35 (EXP-0033 ref)** | **0.749** | **−0.006** | **[−0.011,−0.001]** | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.50** | **0.696** | **+0.046** | **[+0.042,+0.051]** | **MONITOR wins (robust)** ← FLIP |\012| 0.70 | 0.635 | +0.108 | [+0.102,+0.113] | monitor wins |\012| 0.90 | 0.587 | +0.156 | [+0.149,+0.161] | monitor wins |\012| 1.20 | 0.537 | +0.206 | [+0.199,+0.213] | monitor wins |\012| 1.60 | 0.495 | +0.248 | [+0.240,+0.256] | monitor wins |\012| 2.00 | 0.469 | +0.274 | [+0.266,+0.282] | monitor wins |\012\012**FLIP-POINT: sigma_conf ≈ 0.50** (equal==beat: the loss flips straight to a robust win between\0120.35 and 0.50). The flip corresponds to the confidence baseline's discrimination dropping from\012AUC 0.749 → **0.696**. So the monitor wins once the agent's own self-confidence has failure-detection\012**AUROC below ~0.72**.\012\012### Is that flip-point within realistic published LLM self-confidence miscalibration? → YES (directionally).\012Verbalized/self-reported LLM confidence on hard multi-step/agentic tasks is repeatedly reported with\012WEAK failure discrimination, frequently in the AUROC ~0.5–0.70 band:\012- **Agentic Uncertainty Reveals Agentic Overconfidence** (arXiv 2602.06948): agents that succeed 22%\012  of the time predict 77% — severe overconfidence, weak success/failure discrimination.\012- **Benchmarking LLM Confidence in Clinical Questions** (PubMed 40378406): "even the most accurate\012  models show **minimal variation in confidence between right and wrong answers**" → AUROC ≈ 0.5.\012- **The Confidence Dichotomy** (arXiv 2601.07264): tool-use agents show **severe verbalized\012  overconfidence**, esp. with evidence/search tools.\012- **Mind the Confidence Gap** (arXiv 2502.11028): large overconfidence / high ECE documented broadly.\012So a confidence-AUC of ~0.70 (the level at which the monitor begins to win) is well within — even\012optimistic relative to — commonly reported *verbalized* confidence discrimination. **On this axis the\012claim looks L1-worthy.** BUT this is a coarse sim mapping (sigma_conf is a stand-in for readout noise,\012not measured LLM logprobs), and it is OVERTURNED by the prior-art finding below — and by a competing\012strong-confidence regime: when confidence is taken from *step-by-step self-evaluation / logprob\012probes* rather than naive verbalized scores, reported AUC is **0.9+** (see Blocker 2), i.e.\012sigma_conf well below the flip-point, where the monitor LOSES.\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 2 — LIVE ≥2-SOURCE PRIOR-ART CHECK (egress available; arxiv reachable, verified live)\012\012The surviving weak-form novelty was: *"an EARLY ONLINE causal failure signature with measured\012LEAD TIME, distinct from difficulty."* Live check of the three named systems + adjacent 2025–26 work:\012\0121. **Step-level PRMs / Let's Verify Step by Step** (Lightman et al. 2023, arXiv 2305.20050) — VERIFIED.\012   Step-level scoring IS online/causal (scores step ≤ t). It does NOT measure lead-time-before-failure\012   and is trained on reasoning correctness, not agent-trajectory failure. Partial overlap (online\012   step scoring) but not lead-time framing. NOT a full relabel by itself.\012\0122. **AgentBoard progress-rate** (Ma et al., NeurIPS 2024, OpenReview 09Y7J22N9c) — VERIFIED.\012   "Progress rate" = max fraction of annotated subgoals achieved along a trajectory. It is an\012   **analytical EVALUATION metric requiring ground-truth subgoal annotations**, reported over\012   completed/truncated rollouts (post-hoc), NOT an online causal failure predictor on live unseen\012   trajectories and NOT lead-time-measured. So AgentBoard alone does NOT fully relabel the online\012   lead-time claim — but it occupies the "online progression score" conceptual territory.\012\0123. **Online LLM-as-Judge / stepwise self-evaluation** — VERIFIED, and this is the KILL:\012   - **Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure\012     Detection** (Mavi et al., arXiv 2511.07364, Nov 2025). Does **step-by-step (online-capable)\012     confidence scoring for FAILURE DETECTION on multi-step tasks**, AUC-ROC up to ~0.9, stepwise\012     beats holistic by up to +15% rel. This is precisely "online stepwise scoring of impending\012     failure on multi-step agent trajectories." It IS the self-confidence baseline done online and\012     done well (AUC ~0.9, i.e. sigma_conf far below our flip-point → monitor would LOSE).\012   - **Accurate Failure Prediction in Agents...** (Vasudev et al., arXiv 2602.03338, Feb 2026):\012     a binary LLM **critic** with offline **AUROC 0.94** predicting agent failure — and shows the\012     deeper problem that accurate prediction does not imply effective prevention.\012\012### Prior-art verdict\012The conceptual space — **online/stepwise scoring of impending failure on multi-step LLM-agent\012trajectories** — is OCCUPIED (2511.07364 directly; PRMs and AgentBoard adjacent). The ONLY sliver\012left for CLAIM-0037 is the *specific framing* "an EXTERNAL causal-observable signature (tool-error +\012looping − info-gain) with MEASURED LEAD TIME that beats BOTH self-confidence AND difficulty." But:\012- EXP-0033 already showed it **does not beat self-confidence** at realistic-strong calibration, and\012  the published online self-evaluators hit AUC ~0.9 (strong calibration → our model says monitor loses).\012- The **lead-time** half was already weak in EXP-0033 (only 42% of failures flagged ≥1 step early,\012  below the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The novelty hinges on a property the L0 itself failed.\012- "Distinct from difficulty" (the one robust win, +0.095) is real but is the **weakest, least novel**\012  half — process-vs-difficulty separation is implicit in any step-level PRM / progress-rate work.\012\012The claim's distinctive contribution is therefore **relabeling** of online stepwise failure\012detection, dressed in a lead-time framing the L0 cannot support.\012\012---\012\012## DECISION RULE (PRE-REGISTERED) → outcome\012> IF flip-point within realistic miscalibration AND no published online lead-time scorer -> L1-worthy.\012> ELSE -> converge.\012\012- Flip-point within realistic *verbalized*-confidence range? **YES.**\012- No published online lead-time trajectory failure scorer? **FALSE** — 2511.07364 does online stepwise\012  failure detection (AUC ~0.9); PRMs/AgentBoard occupy the progression-score territory.\012\012Because the AND fails on the prior-art conjunct, the pre-registered rule fires the ELSE branch:\012**CONVERGE.** Additionally, even the flip-point favorability is fragile: the published online\012self-evaluators achieve AUC ~0.9 (sigma_conf ≪ flip), the regime where our own model says the monitor\012LOSES. The monitor only wins against *naive verbalized* confidence, not against the *online stepwise\012self-evaluation* that the prior art already deploys.\012\012## RECOMMENDATION: RED / weaken-to-relabeling.\012Do NOT spend a GPU L1. The strong claim ("cheap external online monitor beats the agent's own signal\012AND difficulty, with lead time") is (a) dominated by published online stepwise self-evaluation\012(AUC ~0.9), and (b) its surviving lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (42% coverage). The only\012robust positive — "progression signal is distinct from static difficulty" — is real but is the\012least-novel half and is implicit in existing PRM/progress-rate work. This cheap L0 did its job:\012it AVOIDED a wasted GPU L1.\012\012## ARTIFACTS\012- PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed before run, HEAD c5998f9)\012- sweep.py (imports EXP-0033/exp.py unchanged)\012- results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv, results/flip_summary.csv\012- logs/sweep.log\012\012## EXP-0033 (original L0) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0033 (CLAIM-0037)\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0033 | **L0** CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock ~2.6s (budget 15min)\012**Design:** pre-registered in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running). Sim/trace L0 modeling study.\012**Sweep:** 12 cells (frac_foreshadowed × sigma × rho) × 8 seeds = 96 rows; 400 trajectories/row.\012Raw: `results/raw_results.csv` · per-cell: `results/agg_by_cell.csv` · global: `results/global_summary.csv`\012\012## VERDICT: PARTIAL → effectively NEGATIVE on the load-bearing test\012\012The online causal monitor passes **2 of 3** required gates but FAILS the one that matters most for the\012claim as written (it must beat BOTH baselines).\012\012| Gate | Result | Pass? |\012|---|---|---|\012| (A) Lead time exists (>=1 step before agent termination) | median lead **+2.32 steps**; but only **42%** of failures flagged >=1 step early at 30% flag-rate (pre-reg bar was >0.5) | PARTIAL |\012| (B1) Beats GENERIC DIFFICULTY (anti-relabeling, the CLAIM-0015 death) | AUC(mon)-AUC(diff) = **+0.095**, 95% CI **[+0.087, +0.103]** (excludes 0); 96/96 rows positive | **YES** |\012| (B2) Beats AGENT SELF-CONFIDENCE | AUC(mon)-AUC(conf) = **-0.006**, 95% CI **[-0.011, -0.001]** (below 0) | **NO** |\012| Anti-circularity: monitor < latent-health ORACLE | mon 0.743 < oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115) | YES |\012\012**Bottom line:** The progression signature is REAL and is NOT merely a relabeling of intrinsic\012difficulty — it decisively beats a generic difficulty predictor with tight CIs, and it carries genuine\012lead time (~2.3 steps median). BUT it does NOT beat the agent's own self-confidence. In this model the\012agent's confidence is a (noisy) readout of the same latent health the failure is generated from, so it is\012structurally privileged: an online observer reconstructing health from indirect noisy proxies cannot do\012better than a direct (noisy) readout of that health. The claim requires beating BOTH (a) and (b); it\012beats (b) but ties/loses to (a). **Per the pre-registered honest-negative branch, this is reported as a\012(partial) NEGATIVE.**\012\012## (A) LEAD-TIME DISTRIBUTION\012- Online monitor median lead = **+2.32 steps** before the agent's own termination (pooled across cells).\012- It is even slightly EARLIER than self-confidence's first-fire (~1.6 steps) at matched 30% flag-rate.\012- HOWEVER, coverage is the catch: only **42.4%** of failed trajectories are flagged >=1 step early\012  (the rest are flagged late or never within budget) — below the pre-registered >0.5 bar.\012- Trend with foreshadowing: as frac_foreshadowed rises 0.2 -> 0.8, frac-early climbs 0.37 -> 0.48 and\012  AUC climbs 0.69 -> 0.80, confirming the signature tracks foreshadowed (not abrupt) failure. When abrupt\012  failures dominate (frac=0.2) the monitor's edge shrinks toward the noise floor, exactly as pre-registered.\012\012## (B) HEAD-TO-HEAD AT MATCHED FLAG-RATE (the anti-relabeling test)\012- vs generic difficulty: **WIN, robust.** d=+0.095, CI [+0.087,+0.103], all 96 rows positive. The online\012  progression signal contains information the static difficulty predictor does not. This is the test\012  CLAIM-0015 failed; here the monitor clears it cleanly.\012- vs self-confidence: **LOSS/TIE, robust.** d=-0.006, CI [-0.011,-0.001]. Small but consistently negative.\012  Self-confidence (direct noisy health readout) >= reconstructed-from-observables monitor.\012\012## ORACLE GAP (anti-circularity, the CLAIM-0008/0013 lesson)\012Monitor AUC 0.743 sits well below the latent-health oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115). The monitor is NOT secretly\012reading the label — it operates on emitted noisy observables only and pays the reconstruction penalty.\012Confidence (0.749) also sits below the oracle. Anti-circularity holds.\012\012## HONEST INTERPRETATION\012The model is built so the agent's OWN confidence is a privileged direct (noisy) view of latent health,\012while the monitor must reconstruct health from indirect proxies (tool-error/loop/info-gain). Under that\012(arguably realistic) assumption, the online monitor cannot beat self-confidence — it can only beat the\012difficulty baseline. The claim's strong form ("beats BOTH") is therefore FALSIFIED in this regime.\012A weaker, still-interesting form survives: **the online progression signature beats a generic difficulty\012baseline and has real lead time** — i.e., impending failure has a structural progression signature\012distinct from intrinsic difficulty (the anti-relabeling claim holds). But it is dominated by the agent's\012own self-report, which undercuts the practical "cheap external monitor beats the agent" pitch.\012\012This conclusion is sensitive to ONE modeling assumption: how good is real-agent self-confidence? Published\012work suggests LLM self-confidence/verbalized-uncertainty is often POORLY calibrated and weakly predictive\012of failure — if real self-confidence is noisier than modeled here (larger sigma_conf, or biased), the\012monitor could flip to beating it. That is the key L1 question, not resolvable at L0.\012\012## WHAT A REAL L1 SHOULD MEASURE\012- Real agent FAILURE trajectories: tau-bench, AppWorld, SWE-agent rollouts (success + failure).\012- Real online features: actual tool-error/retry events, action n-gram self-similarity (true looping),\012  new-entity/new-token info-gain per step, step index — extracted causally (no peeking past step t).\012- Real lead time vs the agent's ACTUAL termination/error step (not a simulated death threshold).\012- Real self-confidence baseline: the agent's verbalized confidence / logprob-derived uncertainty,\012  with its TRUE (mis)calibration — this is the load-bearing comparison and the one L0 cannot settle.\012- Real difficulty baseline: a per-task difficulty predictor trained on task features only (no trajectory).\012- Then re-run (B): does the online monitor beat self-confidence AND difficulty at matched flag-rate with\012  real lead time? L0 says it beats difficulty but not (modeled) confidence.\012\012## PRIOR-ART CAVEAT\012Agent-failure attribution, LLM-as-judge post-hoc trajectory analysis, AgentBoard, and trajectory-eval are\012PUBLISHED — but they are POST-HOC (analyze a finished trajectory). The novelty would be the EARLY ONLINE\012CAUSAL signature with MEASURED lead time beating self-confidence AND difficulty. L0 supports the\012"distinct-from-difficulty + has lead time" half but NOT the "beats the agent's own signal" half. Failure\012analysis itself is well-trodden; honest framing required.\012\012## committee#1 reference: VERDICT-0034 yellow (required exactly this sweep + prior-art check).\012=== END PACKET ===\012Output ONLY your structured vote block for role systems_reviewer.
./experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0034/committee2/systems_reviewer.err:707:  501 36387     1   0  3:10AM ??         0:00.00 /usr/local/bin/dotslash /usr/local/bin/codex_cli/codex.real -c model_providers.responses.name="Azure" -c model_providers.responses.env_key="OPENAI_API_KEY" -c model_providers.responses.wire_api="responses" -c model_providers.responses.base_url="http://plugboardv2.x2p.facebook.net/codex/passthrough/v1" -c web_search="disabled" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-x2pagentd-inject-cat="eyJ2ZXJpZmllciI6ICJtZXRhbWF0ZV9wbGF0Zm9ybS5wbHVnYm9hcmQiLCAidG9rZW5UaW1lb3V0U2Vjb25kcyI6IDMwMCwgImlzTG93Qm94IjogdHJ1ZX0=" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.X-Meta-Codex-Invocation-Id="codex_invocation_b91902e9-e4c2-419e-8c5a-31b9db18724a" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-client-id="codex_cli" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-meta-user-fbid="771758239291063" -c model_providers.responses.http_headers.x-meta-commit-id="6c231c8c3674c65e67ccae6553487b2b504f1a45" exec --skip-git-repo-check # Systems Reviewer — v001\012Read _committee_common_v001.md + _shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012MANDATE: is this a systems INSIGHT/DESIGN, or mere engineering integration? Search OSS (vLLM/SGLang/\012LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM/etc.) for whether existing systems already implement this mechanism.\012CHECK: does a shipping system already do this; is the contribution an insight or just glue; does it hold\012at realistic scale/workload; reproducibility of the artifact.\012OUTPUT:\012```\012ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green|yellow|red\012EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [{system, url, overlap}]\012CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight | design | mechanism | integration-only\012SCALE_REALISM: <holds at realistic workload? gaps?>\012RATIONALE: <cite>\012```\012# Hostile Committee — common rules (v001)\012You are ONE reviewer in a 6-member hostile committee. You review a COMPRESSED review packet (claim,\012stage, hypothesis, evidence, negative evidence, closest prior work, mandatory baselines, artifacts,\012previous verdict summary, decision requested) — NOT long session logs. You have an INDEPENDENT context.\012\012RULES (all members):\012- Source of truth = the packet's cited evidence + your own prior-art search via prompts/_shared/TOOLING_v001.md.\012- ANTI-COPING: "novel/promising/significant/structural/impactful" FORBIDDEN unless immediately followed by a\012  cited metric or named prior work (with retrievable URL/citation).\012- Reason ONLY from evidence. If evidence is missing, say "insufficient evidence", do not assume.\012- Hostile, not cynical: a clean negative result or honest YELLOW is a valid, valuable outcome.\012- Output your vote in the structured block your role defines; the Area Chair aggregates. Do NOT mutate the\012  academic map directly — propose map deltas.\012- Record the prompt version you ran (v001) so verdicts are auditable.\012VOTE VALUES: green | yellow | red  (+ your role-specific findings). Consensus: GREEN=all green; any yellow\012(no red)=yellow; any red=red.\012\012=== REVIEW PACKET ===\012# COMMITTEE#2 — CLAIM-0037 (PROJ-0008), WITH cheap-L0 follow-up (EXP-0033 L0 + EXP-0034 sigma_conf-sweep+prior-art)\012## SECOND pass. committee#1 (VERDICT-0034) voted yellow and required (cheap, L0-budget, BEFORE any GPU L1): a sigma_conf flip-point sweep + a live prior-art check vs PRM/AgentBoard/online-LLM-as-Judge. EXP-0034 did BOTH. Cast FINAL votes. Engine enforces real 6/6; GREEN only if truly unanimous green by role — NEVER fabricate. The follow-up recommends RED/relabeling — vote honestly.\012\012## EXP-0034 KEY FINDINGS (the 2 committee#1 blockers, resolved):\012(1) sigma_conf FLIP-POINT: the monitor's AUC (0.743) is INDEPENDENT of sigma_conf (never reads confidence); only the self-confidence baseline degrades. Flip-point sigma_conf ~= 0.50 — the monitor starts winning once the agent's self-confidence failure-detection AUROC drops below ~0.72. On the VERBALIZED-confidence axis this IS within realistic LLM miscalibration (verbalized confidence AUROC ~0.5-0.70 on hard agentic tasks — Agentic Overconfidence arXiv 2602.06948 [22%-success agents predict 77%]; clinical bench AUROC~0.5; Confidence Dichotomy 2601.07264; Mind the Confidence Gap 2502.11028). On THIS axis alone the claim looked L1-worthy.\012(2) PRIOR-ART (LIVE-verified, egress available) — the OVERTURN: Mavi et al. 2511.07364 'Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure Detection' (Nov 2025) ALREADY does online stepwise failure detection on multi-step agent trajectories at AUC-ROC ~0.9 (stepwise beats holistic +15% rel). Vasudev et al. 2602.03338: LLM critic predicting agent failure at AUROC 0.94. AgentBoard progress-rate (Ma NeurIPS24) = post-hoc subgoal eval (partial). PRMs/Lets-Verify (Lightman 2023) = online step scoring (partial). => the online-stepwise-failure-detection space is OCCUPIED, and at the strong calibration those systems achieve (~0.9), CLAIM-0037's OWN model says the monitor LOSES.\012\012## SYNTHESIS: the monitor only beats NAIVE verbalized confidence — not the online stepwise self-eval the prior art already deploys at ~0.9 AUC. The lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (only 42% of failures flagged >=1 step early, < the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The one robust positive (+0.095 vs static difficulty) is the LEAST-novel half (implicit in any PRM/progress-rate work). Strong claim = RELABELING of online stepwise failure detection dressed in an unsupported lead-time framing. The pre-registered decision rule's ELSE branch fired (flip realistic on verbalized axis BUT a published online lead-time-capable scorer EXISTS). Recommend RED. Do NOT spend a GPU L1 — the cheap L0 correctly avoided that waste.\012\012## CLAIM\012claim: "In multi-step LLM agent trajectories that ultimately FAIL, the failure is\\012  \ foreshadowed by a CAUSAL, online-observable signature (e.g. rising tool-error/retry\\012  \ rate, repeated near-identical actions = looping, declining new-information-per-step)\\012  \ that a cheap monitor can detect to flag the trajectory as doomed STRICTLY EARLIER\\012  \ (>=N steps before) than the agent's own termination/error \u2014 AND this early-warning\\012  \ signal beats BOTH (a) the agent's own self-reported confidence and (b) a generic\\012  \ per-trajectory difficulty predictor, because impending agentic failure has a structural\\012  \ progression signature distinct from intrinsic task difficulty."\012why_it_matters: "FRESH axis (agentic failure-attribution / early-warning \u2014 untouched\\012\012## EXP-0034 (follow-up) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0034 (CLAIM-0037) — CHEAP L0 follow-up to EXP-0033\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0034 | L0 CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock **7.9s** (budget 15min)\012**Pre-registered** in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running, HEAD c5998f9).\012Reuses EXP-0033's model UNCHANGED (imports `exp.py`). Two blockers Committee#1 demanded.\012\012## VERDICT: CONVERGE → recommend RED/weaken. The claim is, in its strong load-bearing form, RELABELING.\012\012Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient:\0121. **Flip-point analysis is favorable but NOT decisive for L1** — and is dominated by reason 2.\0122. **Prior-art kill:** published systems already do online stepwise failure detection on multi-step\012   tasks, AND the surviving "lead-time" novelty is not actually demonstrated by this L0 (lead-time\012   coverage already failed in EXP-0033 at 42% < 0.5).\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 1 — sigma_conf FLIP-POINT SWEEP (results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv)\012\012Monitor AUC is **independent** of sigma_conf (0.7427, never reads confidence); only the confidence\012baseline degrades as its readout noise grows. **Reproducibility check passed:** the sigma_conf=0.35\012row exactly reproduces EXP-0033 (d_mon_conf = −0.006, CI [−0.0105, −0.0013]).\012\012| sigma_conf | AUC(conf) | d=AUC(mon)−AUC(conf) | 95% CI | monitor verdict |\012|---|---|---|---|---|\012| 0.10 | 0.822 | −0.079 | [−0.083,−0.075] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| 0.20 | 0.797 | −0.055 | [−0.059,−0.051] | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.35 (EXP-0033 ref)** | **0.749** | **−0.006** | **[−0.011,−0.001]** | confidence wins (robust) |\012| **0.50** | **0.696** | **+0.046** | **[+0.042,+0.051]** | **MONITOR wins (robust)** ← FLIP |\012| 0.70 | 0.635 | +0.108 | [+0.102,+0.113] | monitor wins |\012| 0.90 | 0.587 | +0.156 | [+0.149,+0.161] | monitor wins |\012| 1.20 | 0.537 | +0.206 | [+0.199,+0.213] | monitor wins |\012| 1.60 | 0.495 | +0.248 | [+0.240,+0.256] | monitor wins |\012| 2.00 | 0.469 | +0.274 | [+0.266,+0.282] | monitor wins |\012\012**FLIP-POINT: sigma_conf ≈ 0.50** (equal==beat: the loss flips straight to a robust win between\0120.35 and 0.50). The flip corresponds to the confidence baseline's discrimination dropping from\012AUC 0.749 → **0.696**. So the monitor wins once the agent's own self-confidence has failure-detection\012**AUROC below ~0.72**.\012\012### Is that flip-point within realistic published LLM self-confidence miscalibration? → YES (directionally).\012Verbalized/self-reported LLM confidence on hard multi-step/agentic tasks is repeatedly reported with\012WEAK failure discrimination, frequently in the AUROC ~0.5–0.70 band:\012- **Agentic Uncertainty Reveals Agentic Overconfidence** (arXiv 2602.06948): agents that succeed 22%\012  of the time predict 77% — severe overconfidence, weak success/failure discrimination.\012- **Benchmarking LLM Confidence in Clinical Questions** (PubMed 40378406): "even the most accurate\012  models show **minimal variation in confidence between right and wrong answers**" → AUROC ≈ 0.5.\012- **The Confidence Dichotomy** (arXiv 2601.07264): tool-use agents show **severe verbalized\012  overconfidence**, esp. with evidence/search tools.\012- **Mind the Confidence Gap** (arXiv 2502.11028): large overconfidence / high ECE documented broadly.\012So a confidence-AUC of ~0.70 (the level at which the monitor begins to win) is well within — even\012optimistic relative to — commonly reported *verbalized* confidence discrimination. **On this axis the\012claim looks L1-worthy.** BUT this is a coarse sim mapping (sigma_conf is a stand-in for readout noise,\012not measured LLM logprobs), and it is OVERTURNED by the prior-art finding below — and by a competing\012strong-confidence regime: when confidence is taken from *step-by-step self-evaluation / logprob\012probes* rather than naive verbalized scores, reported AUC is **0.9+** (see Blocker 2), i.e.\012sigma_conf well below the flip-point, where the monitor LOSES.\012\012---\012\012## BLOCKER 2 — LIVE ≥2-SOURCE PRIOR-ART CHECK (egress available; arxiv reachable, verified live)\012\012The surviving weak-form novelty was: *"an EARLY ONLINE causal failure signature with measured\012LEAD TIME, distinct from difficulty."* Live check of the three named systems + adjacent 2025–26 work:\012\0121. **Step-level PRMs / Let's Verify Step by Step** (Lightman et al. 2023, arXiv 2305.20050) — VERIFIED.\012   Step-level scoring IS online/causal (scores step ≤ t). It does NOT measure lead-time-before-failure\012   and is trained on reasoning correctness, not agent-trajectory failure. Partial overlap (online\012   step scoring) but not lead-time framing. NOT a full relabel by itself.\012\0122. **AgentBoard progress-rate** (Ma et al., NeurIPS 2024, OpenReview 09Y7J22N9c) — VERIFIED.\012   "Progress rate" = max fraction of annotated subgoals achieved along a trajectory. It is an\012   **analytical EVALUATION metric requiring ground-truth subgoal annotations**, reported over\012   completed/truncated rollouts (post-hoc), NOT an online causal failure predictor on live unseen\012   trajectories and NOT lead-time-measured. So AgentBoard alone does NOT fully relabel the online\012   lead-time claim — but it occupies the "online progression score" conceptual territory.\012\0123. **Online LLM-as-Judge / stepwise self-evaluation** — VERIFIED, and this is the KILL:\012   - **Self-Evaluating LLMs for Multi-Step Tasks: Stepwise Confidence Estimation for Failure\012     Detection** (Mavi et al., arXiv 2511.07364, Nov 2025). Does **step-by-step (online-capable)\012     confidence scoring for FAILURE DETECTION on multi-step tasks**, AUC-ROC up to ~0.9, stepwise\012     beats holistic by up to +15% rel. This is precisely "online stepwise scoring of impending\012     failure on multi-step agent trajectories." It IS the self-confidence baseline done online and\012     done well (AUC ~0.9, i.e. sigma_conf far below our flip-point → monitor would LOSE).\012   - **Accurate Failure Prediction in Agents...** (Vasudev et al., arXiv 2602.03338, Feb 2026):\012     a binary LLM **critic** with offline **AUROC 0.94** predicting agent failure — and shows the\012     deeper problem that accurate prediction does not imply effective prevention.\012\012### Prior-art verdict\012The conceptual space — **online/stepwise scoring of impending failure on multi-step LLM-agent\012trajectories** — is OCCUPIED (2511.07364 directly; PRMs and AgentBoard adjacent). The ONLY sliver\012left for CLAIM-0037 is the *specific framing* "an EXTERNAL causal-observable signature (tool-error +\012looping − info-gain) with MEASURED LEAD TIME that beats BOTH self-confidence AND difficulty." But:\012- EXP-0033 already showed it **does not beat self-confidence** at realistic-strong calibration, and\012  the published online self-evaluators hit AUC ~0.9 (strong calibration → our model says monitor loses).\012- The **lead-time** half was already weak in EXP-0033 (only 42% of failures flagged ≥1 step early,\012  below the pre-registered 0.5 bar). The novelty hinges on a property the L0 itself failed.\012- "Distinct from difficulty" (the one robust win, +0.095) is real but is the **weakest, least novel**\012  half — process-vs-difficulty separation is implicit in any step-level PRM / progress-rate work.\012\012The claim's distinctive contribution is therefore **relabeling** of online stepwise failure\012detection, dressed in a lead-time framing the L0 cannot support.\012\012---\012\012## DECISION RULE (PRE-REGISTERED) → outcome\012> IF flip-point within realistic miscalibration AND no published online lead-time scorer -> L1-worthy.\012> ELSE -> converge.\012\012- Flip-point within realistic *verbalized*-confidence range? **YES.**\012- No published online lead-time trajectory failure scorer? **FALSE** — 2511.07364 does online stepwise\012  failure detection (AUC ~0.9); PRMs/AgentBoard occupy the progression-score territory.\012\012Because the AND fails on the prior-art conjunct, the pre-registered rule fires the ELSE branch:\012**CONVERGE.** Additionally, even the flip-point favorability is fragile: the published online\012self-evaluators achieve AUC ~0.9 (sigma_conf ≪ flip), the regime where our own model says the monitor\012LOSES. The monitor only wins against *naive verbalized* confidence, not against the *online stepwise\012self-evaluation* that the prior art already deploys.\012\012## RECOMMENDATION: RED / weaken-to-relabeling.\012Do NOT spend a GPU L1. The strong claim ("cheap external online monitor beats the agent's own signal\012AND difficulty, with lead time") is (a) dominated by published online stepwise self-evaluation\012(AUC ~0.9), and (b) its surviving lead-time novelty is unsupported even at L0 (42% coverage). The only\012robust positive — "progression signal is distinct from static difficulty" — is real but is the\012least-novel half and is implicit in existing PRM/progress-rate work. This cheap L0 did its job:\012it AVOIDED a wasted GPU L1.\012\012## ARTIFACTS\012- PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed before run, HEAD c5998f9)\012- sweep.py (imports EXP-0033/exp.py unchanged)\012- results/sweep_sigma_conf.csv, results/flip_summary.csv\012- logs/sweep.log\012\012## EXP-0033 (original L0) RESULTS\012# RESULTS — EXP-0033 (CLAIM-0037)\012\012**Researcher:** researcher-0033 | **L0** CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL | wall-clock ~2.6s (budget 15min)\012**Design:** pre-registered in PRE_REGISTRATION.md (committed BEFORE running). Sim/trace L0 modeling study.\012**Sweep:** 12 cells (frac_foreshadowed × sigma × rho) × 8 seeds = 96 rows; 400 trajectories/row.\012Raw: `results/raw_results.csv` · per-cell: `results/agg_by_cell.csv` · global: `results/global_summary.csv`\012\012## VERDICT: PARTIAL → effectively NEGATIVE on the load-bearing test\012\012The online causal monitor passes **2 of 3** required gates but FAILS the one that matters most for the\012claim as written (it must beat BOTH baselines).\012\012| Gate | Result | Pass? |\012|---|---|---|\012| (A) Lead time exists (>=1 step before agent termination) | median lead **+2.32 steps**; but only **42%** of failures flagged >=1 step early at 30% flag-rate (pre-reg bar was >0.5) | PARTIAL |\012| (B1) Beats GENERIC DIFFICULTY (anti-relabeling, the CLAIM-0015 death) | AUC(mon)-AUC(diff) = **+0.095**, 95% CI **[+0.087, +0.103]** (excludes 0); 96/96 rows positive | **YES** |\012| (B2) Beats AGENT SELF-CONFIDENCE | AUC(mon)-AUC(conf) = **-0.006**, 95% CI **[-0.011, -0.001]** (below 0) | **NO** |\012| Anti-circularity: monitor < latent-health ORACLE | mon 0.743 < oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115) | YES |\012\012**Bottom line:** The progression signature is REAL and is NOT merely a relabeling of intrinsic\012difficulty — it decisively beats a generic difficulty predictor with tight CIs, and it carries genuine\012lead time (~2.3 steps median). BUT it does NOT beat the agent's own self-confidence. In this model the\012agent's confidence is a (noisy) readout of the same latent health the failure is generated from, so it is\012structurally privileged: an online observer reconstructing health from indirect noisy proxies cannot do\012better than a direct (noisy) readout of that health. The claim requires beating BOTH (a) and (b); it\012beats (b) but ties/loses to (a). **Per the pre-registered honest-negative branch, this is reported as a\012(partial) NEGATIVE.**\012\012## (A) LEAD-TIME DISTRIBUTION\012- Online monitor median lead = **+2.32 steps** before the agent's own termination (pooled across cells).\012- It is even slightly EARLIER than self-confidence's first-fire (~1.6 steps) at matched 30% flag-rate.\012- HOWEVER, coverage is the catch: only **42.4%** of failed trajectories are flagged >=1 step early\012  (the rest are flagged late or never within budget) — below the pre-registered >0.5 bar.\012- Trend with foreshadowing: as frac_foreshadowed rises 0.2 -> 0.8, frac-early climbs 0.37 -> 0.48 and\012  AUC climbs 0.69 -> 0.80, confirming the signature tracks foreshadowed (not abrupt) failure. When abrupt\012  failures dominate (frac=0.2) the monitor's edge shrinks toward the noise floor, exactly as pre-registered.\012\012## (B) HEAD-TO-HEAD AT MATCHED FLAG-RATE (the anti-relabeling test)\012- vs generic difficulty: **WIN, robust.** d=+0.095, CI [+0.087,+0.103], all 96 rows positive. The online\012  progression signal contains information the static difficulty predictor does not. This is the test\012  CLAIM-0015 failed; here the monitor clears it cleanly.\012- vs self-confidence: **LOSS/TIE, robust.** d=-0.006, CI [-0.011,-0.001]. Small but consistently negative.\012  Self-confidence (direct noisy health readout) >= reconstructed-from-observables monitor.\012\012## ORACLE GAP (anti-circularity, the CLAIM-0008/0013 lesson)\012Monitor AUC 0.743 sits well below the latent-health oracle 0.858 (gap 0.115). The monitor is NOT secretly\012reading the label — it operates on emitted noisy observables only and pays the reconstruction penalty.\012Confidence (0.749) also sits below the oracle. Anti-circularity holds.\012\012## HONEST INTERPRETATION\012The model is built so the agent's OWN confidence is a privileged direct (noisy) view of latent health,\012while the monitor must reconstruct health from indirect proxies (tool-error/loop/info-gain). Under that\012(arguably realistic) assumption, the online monitor cannot beat self-confidence — it can only beat the\012difficulty baseline. The claim's strong form ("beats BOTH") is therefore FALSIFIED in this regime.\012A weaker, still-interesting form survives: **the online progression signature beats a generic difficulty\012baseline and has real lead time** — i.e., impending failure has a structural progression signature\012distinct from intrinsic difficulty (the anti-relabeling claim holds). But it is dominated by the agent's\012own self-report, which undercuts the practical "cheap external monitor beats the agent" pitch.\012\012This conclusion is sensitive to ONE modeling assumption: how good is real-agent self-confidence? Published\012work suggests LLM self-confidence/verbalized-uncertainty is often POORLY calibrated and weakly predictive\012of failure — if real self-confidence is noisier than modeled here (larger sigma_conf, or biased), the\012monitor could flip to beating it. That is the key L1 question, not resolvable at L0.\012\012## WHAT A REAL L1 SHOULD MEASURE\012- Real agent FAILURE trajectories: tau-bench, AppWorld, SWE-agent rollouts (success + failure).\012- Real online features: actual tool-error/retry events, action n-gram self-similarity (true looping),\012  new-entity/new-token info-gain per step, step index — extracted causally (no peeking past step t).\012- Real lead time vs the agent's ACTUAL termination/error step (not a simulated death threshold).\012- Real self-confidence baseline: the agent's verbalized confidence / logprob-derived uncertainty,\012  with its TRUE (mis)calibration — this is the load-bearing comparison and the one L0 cannot settle.\012- Real difficulty baseline: a per-task difficulty predictor trained on task features only (no trajectory).\012- Then re-run (B): does the online monitor beat self-confidence AND difficulty at matched flag-rate with\012  real lead time? L0 says it beats difficulty but not (modeled) confidence.\012\012## PRIOR-ART CAVEAT\012Agent-failure attribution, LLM-as-judge post-hoc trajectory analysis, AgentBoard, and trajectory-eval are\012PUBLISHED — but they are POST-HOC (analyze a finished trajectory). The novelty would be the EARLY ONLINE\012CAUSAL signature with MEASURED lead time beating self-confidence AND difficulty. L0 supports the\012"distinct-from-difficulty + has lead time" half but NOT the "beats the agent's own signal" half. Failure\012analysis itself is well-trodden; honest framing required.\012\012## committee#1 reference: VERDICT-0034 yellow (required exactly this sweep + prior-art check).\012=== END PACKET ===\012Output ONLY your structured vote block for role systems_reviewer.
